  "query_preview": "..Through tonight...The trough that pulled a cold front \nthrough the region today will quickly lift to the north and east \ntonight. Areas of rain and high elevation snow (above 8000-8500') \nwill end from west to east, with mostly dry conditions expected \novernight. Snow levels are expected to drop below 7000' this \nevening, but by the time they do the majority of the precipitation \nwill have already moved out of the region. A few snowflakes are \npossible around 6000-7000' but outside of the highest elevations \n(Kaibab Plateau and San Francisco Peaks), no accumulations are \nexpected. The strong, gusty southwest winds will gradually diminish \nthis evening before becoming light and variable overnight. The Wind \nAdvisory expires at 8PM MST tonight with no extension needed. Patchy \nfog may develop tonight as low level moisture remains across the \nregion with some areas potentially seeing some dense fog towards \nearly Wednesday morning.  \n\nWednesday...Dry conditions are expected for Wednesday. Temperatures \nwill rebound by a few degrees but remain 5-10 degrees below average. \nExpect southerly winds around 5-15 mph through the afternoon.\n\nThursday through Friday...Dry conditions will continue for the start \nof Thursday before moisture returns and chances for rain and high \nelevation snow showers arrive Thursday afternoon. A low pressure \nsystem will move into the Pacific Northwest on Thursday with the \nbottom of the trough dropping down into Arizona. The trough will \nmove towards the east and exit the region late Friday into Saturday. \nThe system is looking drier than it has over the past few days with \nthe main area of precipitation across the Kaibab Plateau. This \nsystem will bring in colder air with snow levels around 6000-7000'. \nSnow accumulations look to be light with a dusting in spots and the \nKaibab Plateau could see a few inches. This system will also bring \nanother round of strong, gusty south to southwest winds to the \nregion for Thursday through Friday.  \n\nSaturday through early next week... Looking towards the weekend and \ninto early next week, we see the active pattern continuing. \nConditions over the weekend look to remain dry with the potential \nfor another system moving into the area late Sunday or Monday. \nModels continue to waffle with solutions for this system so we will \nneed to monitor it for potential impacts as move through the rest of \nthe week.",
